.TH FFTS 2
.SH NAME
ffts \- the multivariate discrete Fourier transform, in place
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "ffts.m";
ffts := load FFTs FFTs->PATH;

ffts->ffts(a, b, ntot, n, nspan, isn);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Ffts
transforms one variate of a multidimensional complex array in place.
.I A
and
.I b
hold the real and imaginary parts and are replaced by the real and
imaginary parts of the result.
.PP
The array is indexed as Fortran indexes one, without limit on the number
of implied subscripts, and the subroutine is called once for each
variate.
.I Ntot
is the total number of complex values;
.I n
is the dimension of the variate being transformed now; and
.IR nspan / n
is the spacing between consecutive values of that variate.
The sign of
.I isn
selects the sign of the complex exponential, and its magnitude is
normally 1.
.PP
A univariate transform is
.IP
.EX
ffts(a, b, n, n, n, 1);
.EE
.PP
and a trivariate transform of
.IR a ( n1 , n2 , n3 )
is
.IP
.EX
ffts(a, b, n1*n2*n3, n1, n1, 1);
ffts(a, b, n1*n2*n3, n2, n1*n2, 1);
ffts(a, b, n1*n2*n3, n3, n1*n2*n3, 1);
.EE
.PP
The calls for one multivariate transform may be made in any order.
.PP
The data may instead be a single array of alternating real and imaginary
parts, with the magnitude of
.I isn
changed to 2 to give the right indexing increment and
.B a[0:]
and
.B a[1:]
passed for
.I a
and
.IR b .
.PP
There is no scale factor in either direction, so transforming and
transforming back multiplies by
.IR n .
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/math/ffts.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR signal (2),
.IR fftstest (1)
.SH HISTORY
This was R. C. Singleton's mixed-radix algorithm (Stanford Research
Institute, 1968), translated to Limbo by Eric Grosse in 1997.
That translation was wrong: for every length of 8 or more it indexed
past the end of the array it was given and the process died \- n=8
reached element 10 of 8, n=16 element 20 of 16.
It worked for the seven shortest lengths, and since nothing in the tree
ever called it, nobody found out for twenty-nine years.
.PP
The interface is kept, because the Fortran-style multivariate form is
the part worth keeping: a two-dimensional transform is two calls and a
three-dimensional one is three, with no copying and no separate code for
each rank.
The engine underneath is now
.IR signal (2).
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
There is no way to report an error through this interface.
A call whose
.IR ntot ,
.I n
and
.I nspan
do not describe an array \- where
.I n
does not divide
.IR nspan ,
or
.I nspan
does not divide
.IR ntot ,
or the arrays are shorter than the shape claims \- leaves the data
untouched rather than transforming some other array that happens to fit.
.IR Fftstest (1)
checks that it does.
.SH BUGS
The transform is done by copying each variate out into a contiguous
buffer, transforming it and copying it back, rather than in place with
strides.
That is a copy per variate per transform, which the original algorithm
avoided.
.PP
The multivariate form is the only reason to use this rather than
.IR signal (2)
directly, which has the better interface for one dimension.
